course: dictionary
lesson: 13693
title: One word, many meanings
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Some words have more than one meaning, and context tells you which one you need. «The rollercoaster was insane!» The excited tone and the exclamation mark show that insane means amazing or extreme here, not mad.
explain: «Swimming across the icy lake in January is an insane idea.» The word idea is the clue. Try the swap: "a very foolish idea" still makes sense, so insane means very foolish or crazy in this sentence.
explain: Be careful with the oldest meaning. Insane once described people with serious mental illness, but today it is hurtful to use it that way. Say «mental illness» instead, and keep insane for ideas, games and surprises. Phrases help too: «The noise is driving me insane» means the noise is annoying me a lot.
fact: Insane has three main meanings: seriously mentally ill (old and offensive), very foolish or crazy, and amazing or extreme (slang).
fact: «That goal was insane!» means amazing, and this is a friendly slang use.
fact: «That's an insane idea» means very foolish or crazy.
fact: Using insane about people who have a mental illness is hurtful, so say «mental illness» instead.
fact: Phrases: «drive someone insane» means to annoy them a lot, and «an insane amount of» means a very large amount.
exercise: «The rollercoaster was insane!» In this sentence, insane means | Seriously mentally ill | *Amazing and extreme | Very quiet | Slow and boring
exercise: What does insane mean in «Eating ten cakes before lunch is an insane idea»? | Very quiet | Old and dusty | *Very foolish or crazy | Kind and gentle
exercise: Which phrase means «annoy someone a lot»? | *drive someone insane | an insane amount of | an insane idea | an insane rollercoaster
test: Pick the meaning of insane in «That goal was insane!» | Seriously mentally ill | Sad and tired | *Amazing and extreme | Very quiet
test: Which phrase means «a very large amount»? | Drive someone insane | Insane and cold | Insane idea | *An insane amount of
exam: Which sentence uses insane in a correct and kind way? | *«The fireworks were insane!» | «Ben is insane because he has an illness.» | «The sofa is insane and blue.»
